{"name":"Polar Star","hex":"#d0d1cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"polar star","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of pole star."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polar_star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Polar Star (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Polar Star\" is a song by South Korean rock band F.T. Island. It is their ninth single under Warner Music Japan and 12th single overall in Japan. The song was written and composed by Hiroki Horiko. It was released on November 28, 2012, in three editions: a CD-only edition, and limited editions A and B. The single debuted at number four on the Oricon weekly chart, selling over 35,000 copies in its first week."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/16/F.T._Island_-_Polar_Star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_Star_(song)","wikidata":"Q7209048"},{"title":"Polar Star (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Polar Star is a 1989 crime novel by Martin Cruz Smith, set in the Soviet Union in the late 1980s. It is a sequel to Gorky Park and features former militsiya investigator Arkady Renko, taking place during the period of Perestroika."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b3/MCS_PolarStar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_Star_(novel)","wikidata":"Q3972919"},{"title":"USCGC Polar Star","blocks":[{"text":"USCGC Polar Star (WAGB-10) is a United States Coast Guard heavy icebreaker. Commissioned in 1976, the ship was built by Lockheed Shipbuilding and Construction Company of Seattle, Washington, along with sister ship, USCGC Polar Sea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Polar_Star_2.jpg/330px-Polar_Star_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USCGC_Polar_Star","wikidata":"Q4369538"},{"title":"Pole star","blocks":[{"text":"A pole star is a visible star that is approximately aligned with the axis of rotation of an astronomical body; that is, a star whose apparent position is close to one of the celestial poles. On Earth, a pole star would lie directly overhead when viewed from the North or the South Pole."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Star_Trail_above_Beccles_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1855505.jpg/330px-Star_Trail_above_Beccles_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1855505.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pole_star","wikidata":"Q662656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg/250px-AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg/500px-AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg/250px-AleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAleutianIslandsFromSpace.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/UnalaskaAlaska.jpg/330px-UnalaskaAlaska.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/UnalaskaAlaska.jpg/960px-UnalaskaAlaska.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/UnalaskaAlaska.jpg/330px-UnalaskaAlaska.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUnalaskaAlaska.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Uscgc_polar_star.jpg/500px-Uscgc_polar_star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Uscgc_polar_star.jpg/1280px-Uscgc_polar_star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Uscgc_polar_star.jpg/500px-Uscgc_polar_star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The upward angle of Polar Star's bow is designed so that the hull rides up onto the ice surface during icebreaking operations. Subsequently, the ship's weight and forward motion combine to fracture the ice.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUscgc_polar_star.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg/500px-Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg/1280px-Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg/500px-Ocean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_%2847579889531%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Polar Star makes a channel for a cargo ship, 2019","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_Giant_Follows_Polar_Star_(47579889531).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Moored_Seattle.jpg/500px-Moored_Seattle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Moored_Seattle.jpg/1280px-Moored_Seattle.jpg?utm_s